        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Julius Caesar</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">100 BCE – 44 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">12 or 13 July 100 BCE, into the patrician gens Julia, a family that claimed descent from Venus; son of Gaius Julius Caesar and Aurelia of the Aurelii Cottae</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">15 March (the Ides of March) 44 BCE, at a meeting of the Senate in the Curia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>: stabbed at least twenty-three times by a group of senators led by Brutus and Cassius</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Quaestor, 69 BCE; curule aedile, 65 BCE; pontifex maximus from 63 BCE; praetor; governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a>; consul, 59, 48 and 46 BCE; governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a>, Illyricum and Transalpine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> from 58 BCE; dictator from 49 BCE and dictator perpetuo from February 44 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a>; crossing the Rubicon and winning the civil war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>; his Commentaries on the Gallic and civil wars; the title dictator in perpetuity; his assassination on the Ides of March; the name Caesar as a title for emperors, the source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a></span></div>
