    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>From a patrician family of modest influence, Caesar rose through priesthoods, magistracies and an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> of 59 BCE and a command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> that he held for most of a decade.</li><li>Ordered by the Senate to give up his command, he crossed the Rubicon in January 49 BCE, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> and the republican armies in a civil war fought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and concentrated the business of the state in his own person.</li><li>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in perpetuity convinced some sixty senators that no free republic could be restored while he lived; his assassination in 44 BCE led to new civil wars and to the monarchy of his heir Octavian, later Augustus.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>After the defeat of Pharnaces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zela">Zela</a>, Caesar wrote veni, vidi, vici (&quot;I came, I saw, I conquered&quot;). The Roman month Quintilis, in which he was born, was renamed Julius in his honour and is now July.</p></aside>
    </section>

